WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.700
The first film showed each kill entered in two ledgers.

2
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:12.160
Now follow the kills one link further, to the species the prey compete with.

3
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:17.360
You might expect every species a predator eats to do better without it.

4
00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.300
So what happens to a rocky shore when its sea star is gone?

5
00:00:22.383 --> 00:00:28.243
On this rocky shore, space on the rock is a limiting resource: its supply caps the

6
00:00:28.243 --> 00:00:29.663
populations that use it.

7
00:00:30.943 --> 00:00:33.523
Mussels are the strongest competitors for that space.

8
00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:37.847
The sea star Pisaster ochraceus eats mussels.

9
00:00:39.047 --> 00:00:42.407
It also eats barnacles, chitons, limpets and snails.

10
00:00:43.827 --> 00:00:45.807
Follow the orange arrows to each of its prey.

11
00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:53.090
In the early 1960s, Robert Paine began keeping Pisaster off one patch of shore at

12
00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:54.590
Mukkaw Bay, Washington.

13
00:00:56.030 --> 00:01:00.710
He left a neighbouring stretch alone as a control, and surveyed both along line

14
00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:01.710
transects.

15
00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:04.310
The predator is gone from the patch.

16
00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:08.930
Will the number of species holding space on the rock go up, or down?

17
00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:23.347
Down. By his 1966 report, the removal patch held 8 species on the rock instead of

18
00:01:23.347 --> 00:01:27.667
15. The control did not lose species in the same way.

19
00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:30.287
Here is how the patch filled.

20
00:01:31.687 --> 00:01:35.187
Acorn barnacles settled first and took most of the open rock.

21
00:01:36.487 --> 00:01:39.727
Then small mussels and gooseneck barnacles crowded them out.

22
00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:42.167
Algae lost their space.

23
00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:48.410
With Pisaster eating mussels, space kept opening for weaker competitors.

24
00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:51.470
Without it, mussels took the rock.

25
00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:57.690
The sea star raised richness by freeing space, not by eating species that harmed the

26
00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:58.690
rest.

27
00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:00.533
Now look at one chiton.

28
00:02:01.893 --> 00:02:05.233
Pisaster eats chitons, so the sea star costs it some lives.

29
00:02:06.533 --> 00:02:11.153
But the sea star also keeps mussels off the rock, which keeps the chitons' space and

30
00:02:11.153 --> 00:02:12.153
food.

31
00:02:12.517 --> 00:02:14.617
Two arrows, with opposite signs.

32
00:02:15.837 --> 00:02:19.697
Without the sea star, chitons left the patch for lack of space and food.

33
00:02:21.077 --> 00:02:25.517
So here, the sea star's indirect help to some of its own prey outweighed what it

34
00:02:25.517 --> 00:02:26.517
ate.

35
00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:31.240
In a short note three years after his report, Paine used the word keystone.

36
00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:37.460
A keystone species has an effect on its community that is large relative to its

37
00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:38.460
abundance.

38
00:02:38.983 --> 00:02:43.903
Eating the dominant competitor, and so helping weaker ones, is called keystone

39
00:02:43.903 --> 00:02:48.503
predation. But this was one removal patch and one control.

40
00:02:49.743 --> 00:02:53.043
It was a strong local result, not yet a general law.

41
00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:58.767
So a predator's effects can run through the species it eats, to the species they

42
00:02:58.767 --> 00:03:01.947
compete with. On Paine's shore, they did.
